{{Life in Balisca}}

The '''Baliscan housing shortage''' refers to the severe and ongoing deficit in available housing units relative to population needs that has affected Balisca since the 1970s. As of 2022, the shortage was estimated at approximately 2.5 million housing units, representing one of the most significant structural challenges facing Balisca's experimental post-growth oriented economy. The crisis has persisted despite constitutional [[Basic Law: Housing and Shelter|guarantees of housing as a fundamental right]] and the extensive network of [[W:Housing cooperative|housing cooperatives]] and government-funded [[W:Social housing|social housing]], and has created a paradox in which the nation maintains both universal housing entitlement and endemic [[W:Housing insecurity|housing insecurity]]. 

The shortage is disproportionately affecting [[Immigration to Balisca|recent migrants]] and [[W:Refugee|refugees]], exacerbating social tensions regarding migration policy, and has intensified ongoing political conflicts between competing visions of urban development, [[W:Ecological sustainability|ecological sustainability]], and [[W:Economic justice|economic justice]].

International comparisons regarding the Baliscan housing shortage are complicated by the fundamental distinctiveness of the country's [[Economy of Balisca|mutualist economic system]], which defies the market-based metrics typically employed to assess housing affordability in market-driven economies. While the average private market rent of {{Baliscan sol|554|link=yes}} for a one-bedroom apartment in 2023 appears modest by the standards of major global cities, this figure is misleading because access to such units requires either private resources sufficient to bypass the cooperative system or emergency status that places individuals outside the normal allocation queue. 

The more relevant metric of [[W:Housing security|housing security]]—defined as the proportion of the population with secure, long-term access to adequate shelter regardless of market conditions—remains high at approximately 85 percent due to the cooperative system, but the 15 percent experiencing housing insecurity represents over 32 million individuals, a scale of deprivation that would constitute a crisis of historic proportions in most comparable nations.

== Background ==
{{Main|Free migration|Immigration to Balisca|Environmental and Social Accounting Framework}}
The origins of the housing shortage can be traced to the intersection of demographic pressures produced by the [[free migration policy|Policy of Open Frontiers]] adopted in the mid-twentieth century and the [[W:Post-growth economics|post-growth economic framework]] that was ushered in following the [[Special Period]]. Constitutional guarantees of housing for all dates back to the [[Baliscan Revolution]], being adopted as [[Basic Law: Housing and Shelter|Article II]] of the [[Basic Laws of Balisca|Basic Laws]]. The revolutionary constitution guaranteed all citizens access to adequate housing through communal ownership systems, abolishing [[W:Private property|private property]] in land and establishing cantonal housing cooperatives responsible for allocating residential space based on need rather than market capacity. Throughout the early and mid-twentieth century, this system functioned effectively, with construction rates keeping pace with the gradual natural increase of the native-born population. 

However, the 1950s marked a turning point as Balisca emerged from the [[Great Adonian War]] and reasserted its commitment to providing refuge for displaced populations throughout [[Lurando-Abyala]] and beyond. The resulting surge in immigration, which has seen the Confederation's population grow from approximately 85 million in 1970 to over 216 million in 2026, has overwhelmed the ability of the [[Construction sector in Balisca|construction sector]] to expand housing stock commensurately.

=== Environmental concerns and constraints ===
The structural constraints imposed by the post-growth framework have fundamentally shaped the contours of the housing shortage, creating a distinctively Baliscan crisis that differs qualitatively from housing affordability problems in more market-driven economies. The [[Green Protocol]] adopted in 1989 established strict ecological limits on construction materials and methods, prioritizing retrofitting of existing structures over new greenfield development and requiring carbon-neutral or carbon-negative approaches to all new building projects. These regulations, while essential to the Confederation's environmental commitments, have significantly extended construction timelines and increased the resource costs of housing production. A standard cooperative housing block that required eighteen months to complete in 1980 now typically requires four to six years under current environmental standards, even as population growth has accelerated.

Compounding these production constraints, successive confederal government have adopted increasingly stringent anti-sprawl policies throughout the 1990s and 2000s under pressure from ecological organizations including [[Ecological Action]] and allied movements. The so-called [[Development Principles Initiative]] of 1994 established the "up, not out" mandate that restricted [[W:Urban sprawl|horizontal expansion]] of urban areas, instead mandating [[W:Verticalization|vertical densification]] of existing settlements through high-rise construction and [[W:Infill development|infill development]]. While this approach has preserved much of Balisca's extensive agricultural lands and forest ecosystems from suburban encroachment, it has created severe bottlenecks in housing production due to the technical complexity, extended approval processes, and community consultation requirements associated with vertical construction. The resulting "densification queue" has left thousands of approved housing projects awaiting actual construction commencement, with priority determined by complex algorithms accounting for need, waiting time, and ecological impact that often delay new units for years after they enter the planning system.

== Impact ==
The cooperative allocation system, while guaranteeing universal housing entitlement in principle, has generated acute [[W:Scarcity|scarcity]] in practice through the operation of the [[waiting list mechanism]] that governs access to communal housing. As of 2024, the average waiting period for cooperative housing placement in major metropolitan areas ranges from four to seven years in [[Castejón]], [[Alessandria]], and [[Concepción]], to approximately two years in smaller regional centers such as [[Navassa]] and [[Uxiu]]. 

During this interim period, individuals and families must either remain in existing communal arrangements—typically [[W:Extended family|multigenerational family households]]—or seek accommodation in the limited private rental market that operates alongside the cooperative sector. This private market, comprising approximately fifteen percent of Balisca's housing stock, has experienced severe price inflation due to the fundamental mismatch between supply and demand, with average rents for one-bedroom apartments in city centers reaching S/554 in 2023 despite average monthly net salaries of only {{Baliscan sol|2277|link=yes}}. The [[Confederal Institute of Statistics]] has documented that private rents in high-demand urban areas consume between twenty and thirty percent of household income, significantly exceeding the thresholds considered sustainable under the Confederation's adequacy standards.

=== Social ===
{{Further|Housing in Balisca|Squatting in Balisca|Baliscan values#Immigration reform}}
[[File:El Pobleno, Concepcion.jpg|thumb|A [[W:Squatting|squatted]] building in [[Concepción]]'s [[El Pobleno]] barrio, known for its long history of [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] [[W:Direct action|direct action]]]]
The social consequences of the housing shortage manifest most starkly among the Confederation's migrant and refugee populations, who face particular barriers to accessing both cooperative placement and private market housing. A 2022 study by the [[Confederal Institute for Migration Research]] found that more than three-fourths of the estimated 1.3 million inadequately sheltered persons in Balisca—defined as those sleeping in [[W:Self-managed social centers|autonomous social centers]], informal encampments, or [[W:Shanty settlements|shanty settlements]]—were recent migrants who had not yet secured cooperative queue positions or established the [[W:Social network|social networks]] necessary to access communal housing. This phenomenon has given rise to distinctive patterns of housing insecurity concentrated near [[W:Points of entry|points of entry]], including airports, maritime ports, and land border crossings, where newly arrived migrants frequently establish temporary encampments while awaiting processing and placement. The visibility of these encampments has generated significant political tension regarding the [[free migration policy]], with opposition movements arguing that the Confederation's hospitality obligations exceed its infrastructural capacity while defenders maintain that the shortage results from policy failures rather than migration itself.

The housing shortage has produced profound transformations in household formation patterns and intimate relationships throughout Baliscan society, as many have been forced to adapt to extended delays in securing independent housing. The average age at which young adults leave their parental or extended family households has increased steadily over the past three decades, reaching twenty-eight years in 2024 compared to twenty-two years in 1990. This prolonged multigenerational cohabitation has generated both cultural continuity and intergenerational tension, as young people delay establishing independent households while maintaining economic and emotional dependence on family networks. Sociologists have documented the emergence of what they term "[[compressed adulthood]]"—a life course phase in which educational completion, workforce entry, marriage, and childbearing all occur within the confined spaces of existing family homes rather than in independent residences.

The shortage has also influenced the revival and adaptation of non-normative domestic arrangements that maximize housing efficiency within the cooperative system. [[Marriage in Balisca|Civil solidarity pacts]] (PACS), [[W:Polyamory|polyamorous]] and [[W:Group marriage|group marriage]] formations have increased significantly since the 1990s, with the [[Confederal Civil Registry]] documenting a threefold increase in multi-party domestic partnerships between 2000 and 2020. While these arrangements are often understood primarily in terms of [[W:Sexual liberation|sexual]] and emotional liberation, ethnographic research has demonstrated that housing economics constitute a significant motivator for many participants, who explicitly cite the desire to access larger cooperative units or combine queue positions to secure earlier placement. The cooperative allocation system recognizes these formations as legitimate family arrangements eligible for priority placement, creating incentives for relationship forms that maximize the number of petitioners per housing unit. Similarly, the practice of [[solidarity hosting]]—in which established cooperative residents are required to house refugees or migrants in spare rooms until permanent placement—has become increasingly institutionalized, with cantonal regulations in [[Castejón]], [[Merced]], and [[Alessandria]] mandating that households with excess bedroom capacity participate in hosting programs or face reassignment to smaller units.

=== Political debate and immigration controversy ===
{{See also|2018 Baliscan premiership election|Razón y Justicia|Conservatism in Balisca}}
[[File:Josuè Subirà.png|thumb|420x420px|[[Josuè Subirà]], founder of the [[Razón y Justicia]] campaigned from a left-conservative position in the [[2018 Baliscan premiership election|2018]] and [[2024 Baliscan premiership election|2024 premiership elections]], calling for a halt to all immigration until the housing shortage is resolved, and raising eligibility requirements for immigrants for communal housing.]]
Ecological constraints on construction have generated sophisticated debates within the Confederation regarding the appropriate relationship between environmental sustainability and housing justice. The [[W:YIMBY|YIMBY]] ("Yes In My Backyard") movement, which emerged in the early 2000s among urban professionals and architectural cooperatives, advocates for aggressive densification and streamlined approval processes to accelerate housing production, arguing that denser cities represent the only ecologically viable approach to accommodating [[W:Population growth|population growth]]. This position has been opposed by [[W:Degrowth|degrowth]] advocates associated with the [[Limites]] network, who contend that continued population increase itself (both natural and [[Immigration to Balisca|continued immigration]]) constitutes an ecological impossibility (provoking accusations of [[W:Malthusianism|Malthusianism]]) regardless of construction efficiency and who demand instead the redistribution of existing housing stock through stricter occupancy standards. These debates have played out in cantonal assemblies throughout Balisca, with different communities adopting varying balances between densification and redistribution approaches.

The political geography of the housing shortage reveals significant regional disparities that complicate efforts to address the crisis through centralized policy. [[Levante, Balisca|Levantine cities]] with high population densities and strong migration flows—including [[Castejón]], [[Alessandria]], and [[Concepción]]—experience housing insecurity rates approximately five times those of West Coast, despite similar levels of absolute migration flows. This disparity reflects the differential availability of developable land and the varying intensity of anti-sprawl regulations across cantonal boundaries, with some jurisdictions maintaining stricter preservation regimes than others. The resulting pattern of [[housing accessibility inequality]] has generated migration within the Confederation itself, as residents unable to secure placement in high-demand coastal cities relocate to interior communities where waiting lists are shorter, potentially exacerbating labor market imbalances and straining social services in receiving areas.

A [[W:Grey market|grey market]] in housing queue positions has emerged as an unintended consequence of the cooperative allocation system, despite formal prohibition on market transactions in housing entitlements. While legally non-transferable, cooperative queue positions have become objects of informal exchange through arrangements involving employment favors, domestic services, or other non-monetary compensation. The [[Confederal Cooperative Tribunal]] has issued numerous rulings attempting to suppress these practices while acknowledging the structural pressures that generate them, but enforcement remains inconsistent across cantons. More formalized is the practice of [[Housing exchange|housing exchanges]] between households in different locations, in which families trade queue positions or actual units to relocate for employment or family reasons, a practice that operates in a quasi-legal status that authorities generally tolerate despite its technical violation of allocation regulations.

== Response ==

=== Confederal government ===
The [[Casajuan Plan]] represents the most ambitious attempt to address the housing shortage through centralized action, proposing the construction of approximately 3.2 million new cooperative housing units over a fifteen-year period beginning in 2020. The plan, named after it's author [[Raïmon Casajuan]], would require quadrupling the current rate of housing production from approximately 450,000 units annually to over 1.8 million units per year while maintaining [[Green Protocol]] and [[Environmental and Social Accounting Framework]] standards. 

Implementation has been hampered by material shortages, skilled labor constraints, and ongoing debates regarding the ecological implications of such massive construction activity. As of 2024, the plan remained approximately 40 percent behind its targeted production schedule, and independent analysts have concluded that Balisca would need to maintain accelerated production rates for at least seven additional years beyond the original timeline merely to eliminate the existing shortage, without accounting for ongoing population growth.

